= Background and Related Work <sec:background>

This chapter places the report between two traditions that rarely meet: multi-robot coordination, where paths, velocities and priorities are computed from geometric models, and the use of large language models (LLMs) and vision-language models (VLMs) as robot planners. The coordinator studied here takes from both: a VLM names a congestion situation and a strategy, and a deterministic layer turns that name into commands that a conventional navigation stack executes (@sec:design). Each section ends with what its body of work leaves open; @sec:bg-comparison compares the closest systems, and @sec:bg-gap states the combined gap. Statements of the form "no published system does X" are bounded by the literature searches made for this project in autumn 2026 and mean "to our knowledge".

== Multi-Agent Path Finding and Congestion-Aware Planning <sec:bg-mapf>

*The MAPF problem.* Multi-agent path finding (MAPF) asks for conflict-free paths that move a team of agents from their starts to their goals on a shared graph. #cite(<stern2019multi>, form: "prose") standardised its definitions, including vertex conflicts (two agents in one place at one time) and swapping conflicts (two agents exchanging places along one edge); the formulation defines no measure of congestion. Classical MAPF is a discrete abstraction: agents occupy one vertex per time step, time advances synchronously, and physical spacing is absorbed into the grid cell.

*Solvers.* Conflict-Based Search (CBS) @sharon2015conflict is the reference optimal solver. Because optimal MAPF is NP-hard, #cite(<barer2014suboptimal>, form: "prose") relaxed CBS into bounded-suboptimal variants, and a second line gives up optimality for speed: PIBT @okumura2022priority plans one step at a time under adaptive priorities for online use with hundreds of agents, and LaCAM\* @okumura2023improving finds solutions quickly and converges to the optimum given more time.

*Lifelong MAPF in warehouses.* A warehouse poses a stream of MAPF instances, because robots receive new goals as soon as they finish old ones. #cite(<ma2017lifelong>, form: "prose") formalised this as multi-agent pickup and delivery (MAPD). Rolling-Horizon Collision Resolution @li2020lifelong resolves collisions only within a bounded horizon and replans periodically; the bounded horizon lets it scale, but the authors show that two agents in a corridor can keep waiting for each other.

*Congestion as an explicit objective.* #cite(<chen2023traffic>, form: "prose") observe that free-flow optimal paths create congestion and guide agents along congestion-avoiding paths instead; guidance graphs @zhang2024guidance optimise such guidance as edge weights; #cite(<asadi2025congestion>, form: "prose") add a learned congestion predictor to online MAPD; and DeepFleet @agaskar2025deepfleet trains foundation models on fleet movement data from Amazon warehouses. #cite(<tang2026late>, form: "prose") used LLM coding agents offline to specialise a warehouse planner for one site; they rediscovered mechanisms such as corridor-exit priorities, but the gains mostly vanished under a different demand mix. A rule such as "the robot leaving a corridor goes first" is therefore a natural classical reference point.

*Gap.* These planners represent congestion numerically and plan the fleet in advance. Once a jam has formed among robots that run their own local planners, a MAPF solver's model (a graph, discrete time, exact execution) no longer matches what the robots are doing. This report does not replace global planning; it asks whether a semantic layer can recognise and clear a formed jam and then hand control back.

== Reactive Collision Avoidance and Navigation Stacks <sec:bg-reactive>

*Reactive methods.* Each robot chooses its next motion from what it senses now. Artificial potential fields @khatib1986real, extended to dynamic environments @ge2002dynamic, are cheap but suffer from local minima; the dynamic window approach @fox1997dynamic searches only velocities the robot can reach in a short window; Reciprocal Velocity Obstacles @vandenberg2008reciprocal and their $n$-body form ORCA @vandenberg2011reciprocal share the avoidance effort between agents that reason alike. Swarm robotics builds on such local rules @brambilla2013swarm.

*Where reactive methods stall.* Local rules decide how to avoid a collision but not who should give way. In the "freezing robot" problem @trautman2010unfreezing a planner decides that every path forward is unsafe and stops although a feasible path exists. For controllers based on control barrier functions (CBFs), #cite(<grover2019deadlock>, form: "prose") and #cite(<grover2022before>, form: "prose") show that deadlock is an equilibrium of the closed-loop dynamics on the boundary of the safe set: the robots stop because continuing would violate safety. The second review also reports that velocity-obstacle methods are empirically prone to deadlock in symmetric scenes, the two-robot aisle and the busy junction that warehouses produce.

*ROS 2 and Nav2.* The robots in this report run ROS 2 @macenski2022robot and its navigation system Nav2 @macenski2020marathon @nav2docs, which plans over costmaps, follows paths with local controllers and runs recovery behaviours when progress fails. Nav2 plans for one robot and sees other robots as costmap obstacles. A design observation of this report, not a claim of the Nav2 authors, is that its recovery behaviours (waiting, backing up, clearing a costmap) are local to one robot, so two robots that face each other and run the same policy can reproduce the stall.

*Layering deliberation over reaction.* The remedies point to a layered design. The provably correct resolution of #cite(<grover2019deadlock>, form: "prose") runs above the reactive controller and acts only once robots are stuck; #cite(<cap2016provably>, form: "prose") replace reactive avoidance with coordinated plans that are only re-timed; Rolling-Horizon Collision Resolution @li2020lifelong interleaves slow planning with execution. In each, a slower layer sets an order or timing and a faster layer keeps motion safe. This report adopts the same split, with a model-based layer in the slow role.

*Gap.* Reactive avoidance and Nav2 give fast, safe local motion, which this report keeps as its local navigation. What they lack is an authority that sees the whole situation and breaks symmetry by choosing an order: who backs off, who passes first, when everyone resumes.

== Deadlock and Congestion: Taxonomies and Resolution Strategies <sec:bg-deadlock>

*Deadlock.* #cite(<coffman1971system>, form: "prose") surveyed the "deadly embrace" of multiprogramming systems. The four conditions associated with that work (mutual exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption, circular wait) carry over to robots once the contested resource is floor space; this mapping is this report's reading, not a result of the survey. It explains why a jam does not dissolve on its own: breaking it means making one robot give up space it holds.

*Congestion as a flow phenomenon.* Congestion is broader than deadlock. Robot swarms moving in single file @alonsollanes2024single reproduce the fundamental diagram of traffic flow, with stop-and-go waves and, at higher density, total congestion; for swarms converging on a target, #cite(<passos2022congestion>, form: "prose") design decentralised algorithms that maximise the target's throughput. These works measure congestion as flow and density over many robots and long intervals.

*Taxonomies of conflict scenes.* For small groups in constrained spaces, the most developed vocabulary is that of social mini-games. #cite(<chandra2023deadlock>, form: "prose") formalise doorways, hallways and intersections as navigation problems among conflicting agents and evaluate success, collision rate, stop time and flow rate; their survey @chandra2025multi argues for a dedicated taxonomy and evaluation protocols. The scenes of this report, a head-on encounter in a narrow aisle and a multi-robot jam at a junction, belong to this family.

*Resolution strategies.* The literature resolves deadlock in four broad ways, each matching a strategy family of this report (@sec:design):

- _Perturbation onto deadlock-free states._ #cite(<grover2019deadlock>, form: "prose") move robots out of deadlock with a provably correct algorithm, and #cite(<chandra2023deadlock>, form: "prose") perturb each robot onto "liveness sets"; both break the symmetry of the encounter on physical robots.
- _Ordering by reservation or priority._ Autonomous intersection management @dresner2008multiagent grants space-time reservations, first come, first served by default; PIBT @okumura2022priority resolves conflicts by an adaptive order.
- _Recede and yield._ A-DRIVE @aoki2022drive detects deadlock among automated vehicles at intersections and single-track segments and has lower-priority vehicles yield and back away, the closest classical analogue of this report's yield-and-back-off manoeuvres.
- _Re-timing instead of re-routing._ Coordinated trajectories stay safe and deadlock-free under delays when only their timing changes @cap2016provably.

Waiting, rerouting and convoying complete the repertoire; whether they help depends on the scene geometry.

*Detection.* A-DRIVE declares deadlock when vehicles wait beyond a threshold while blocking one another @aoki2022drive, and the extended work of Garg et al. @garg2026online triggers on a fleet that has slowed while still far from its goals. Detection is a cheap progress test, and expensive reasoning is reserved for after it fires. This report uses the same division: a stall trigger decides when to call the model, the model proposes, and the grounder decides what is executed.

*Gap.* In classical resolvers, which mechanism applies to which scene is a designer's choice made in advance. The report asks whether a model can make that link online, naming the conflict and picking a strategy from a closed vocabulary that a deterministic layer knows how to execute.

== Language and Vision-Language Models as Robot Planners and Coordinators <sec:bg-vlm>

*From language models to embodied and spatial models.* SayCan @ahn2022grounding combined a language model's judgement of a skill's usefulness with a learned estimate of whether it can succeed. PaLM-E @driess2023palm and RT-2 @brohan2023vision took in perception and actions directly; they are end-to-end, mostly single-robot manipulation models and are background here. Closer to this report are VLMs with stronger spatial reasoning: SpatialVLM @chen2024spatialvlm, and the embodied-reasoning line of Gemini Robotics-ER @gemini2025robotics @gemini2025robotics15, whose later model, Gemini Robotics-ER 2 @google2026geminier2 @google2026geminier, is used in the live experiments. Earlier work and offline analyses used GPT-4o @openai2024system and Claude models @anthropic2026claude. Set-of-Mark prompting @yang2023mark overlays marks on image regions to improve visual grounding; the annotated overhead frame of this report applies the same idea with robot identities drawn on the image.

*LLMs as multi-robot planners.* A first wave plans tasks for robot teams from natural-language instructions, mostly in household and manipulation settings: SMART-LLM @kannan2023smart decomposes and allocates tasks centrally, RoCo @mandi2023roco has per-arm agents negotiate plans with collision-check feedback, COHERENT @liu2024coherent runs a proposal-feedback-adjustment loop between a central assigner and executors, and LaMMA-P @zhang2024lamma has the model write PDDL for a classical planner. #cite(<chen2023scalable>, form: "prose") find a hybrid of centralised and decentralised communication works best in their tasks. The survey of #cite(<li2025large>, form: "prose") names the field's main obstacles: limited mathematical reasoning, hallucination, latency and missing benchmarks. Each shapes this design: the model picks a strategy label instead of computing waypoints, its output passes a closed vocabulary and a schema, it is called only on a stall, and the report builds and registers its own evaluation scenes (Chapter 5). In the terms of #cite(<chen2023scalable>, form: "prose"), the coordinator is centralised only at the coordination layer; each robot keeps its own navigation stack.

*Foundation models for deadlock.* #cite(<garg2024foundation>, form: "prose") let an LLM or VLM resolve deadlocks in connected multi-robot systems by assigning a leader and waypoints that a graph-neural-network policy follows safely; the extended version @garg2026online calls the VLM only when a velocity-based detector fires, prompts it with a top-view image, tests the models' spatial reasoning before planning, and leaves safety to a provably safe controller, in simulation. This line is structurally closest to the present report. LLMDR @seo2025llmdr has an LLM detect deadlock from movement patterns on grid MAPF benchmarks and assign strategies that prioritised planning executes. #cite(<dwedar2026safe>, form: "prose") let a VLM and an LLM propose directives for two heterogeneous robots while a reachability gate alone holds command authority. SwarmVLM @zafar2025swarmvlm uses a VLM under a ceiling camera to tune impedance parameters for a drone leading a ground robot; it does not resolve conflicts between robots.

*Gap.* The multi-robot LLM planners plan tasks from instructions; they do not detect and classify a traffic conflict that arises unprompted. The deadlock works operate on grids with text input (LLMDR) or in simulation with learned controllers (Garg et al.), and the hardware system with a formal gate (Dwedar et al.) contains no congestion scenarios, by the project's reading. As far as we found, none feeds a fixed overhead frame of a warehouse fleet to a VLM to choose among named congestion strategies executed through a standard navigation stack. The report claims no novelty for central deadlock recovery (A-DRIVE, LLMDR, Garg et al.), for a two-rate architecture (Rolling-Horizon Collision Resolution) or for a propose-and-verify loop (RoCo).

== Grounding and Plan Feasibility <sec:bg-grounding>

*Language models do not plan reliably on their own.* #cite(<valmeekam2023planning>, form: "prose") found LLMs limited at generating executable plans autonomously, but useful as heuristic guidance for sound planners and with external verifiers. When a local-search planner repaired LLM seed plans, much of each final plan was due to the planner's edits rather than the model, by the authors' edit-distance comparison: when a deterministic layer repairs a model's plan, credit for the executed plan must be divided. #cite(<kambhampati2024llms>, form: "prose") turn this into "LLM-Modulo" frameworks, in which external model-based verifiers check the model's candidate plans in a loop. For any model choosing from a fixed menu, LLMs also prefer particular option identifiers largely independently of content @zheng2023large.

*Grounding by translation.* LLM+P @liu2023empowering translates a problem into PDDL for a classical planner, and LaMMA-P @zhang2024lamma does so for multi-robot allocation; correctness comes from the planner.

*Grounding by checking and repair.* SayCan @ahn2022grounding filters skills by affordance. The Safety Chip @yang2023plug monitors an LLM agent against temporal-logic constraints and prunes unsafe actions; VerifyLLM @grigorev2025verifyllm checks plans for inconsistencies before execution; RoboGuard @ravichandran2025safety corrects unsafe plans through control synthesis. EvoPlan @nukapotula2026evoplan lets programmatic validators decide which LLM plans survive and grounds them through Nav2 into waypoints checked against a constraint mined from demonstrations, in Gazebo and on a Clearpath Jackal robot. GAVEL @wang2026gavel predicts action effects with a graph world model, repairs what it can and re-prompts for semantic errors. Physical Agentic AI @liu2026physical puts a deterministic orchestrator between an LLM mission planner and a robot crew, authorises one skill at a time, and reports how many faulted steps the gate stops. The reachability gate of #cite(<dwedar2026safe>, form: "prose") and RoCo's feedback @mandi2023roco belong to the same family. Deterministic checking and repair of model plans is therefore established, not a contribution of this report.

*Gap.* This literature mostly checks sequences of symbolic actions for one robot. Whether "robot 2 yields" is feasible instead depends on where the robots stand, how much free space lies behind each, and which way the corridor runs. A coordinator called at a low rate that must never move robots on an unchecked plan cannot rely on rejection or re-prompting alone; the evaluated system uses a geometry-driven grounder that checks, repairs or replaces, refuses, and logs which happened (@sec:design-grounding).

