Three patterns stand out. Most multi-robot language planners work on text, and the image-based systems see a top view built from observations (Garg et al.), one robot's camera (Dwedar et al.) or a ceiling camera used to describe obstacles (SwarmVLM). Every system that moves real robots places something between the model and the actuators, and this report follows that consensus. Checks reject, re-prompt, repair or hand the problem to a planner; Physical Agentic AI reports how often its gate stops faulted steps, but as far as we found none of the compared systems reports, per dispatch, whether the model's plan was kept, repaired or replaced by the checker's own plan.

== Research Gap <sec:bg-gap>

The sections above leave one combined gap. Global planners prevent jams by planning ahead but cannot unpick one formed among robots running their own navigation stacks (@sec:bg-mapf). Reactive avoidance and Nav2 keep robots safe but cannot decide who gives way in a symmetric encounter (@sec:bg-reactive). Classical deadlock resolvers are built for scene families chosen at design time (@sec:bg-deadlock). Language and vision-language planners bring flexible scene understanding but, as far as we found, have not been used to classify and resolve congestion from a fixed overhead camera above a standard navigation stack (@sec:bg-vlm). Plan-checking methods show that model output must be grounded, and several already repair plans deterministically, but they target symbolic single-robot tasks and none reports, per dispatch, how a model's plan fared in the check and whether it changed what was executed (@sec:bg-grounding).

The research questions (@sec:intro-rq) follow from this gap: whether a VLM classifies congestion correctly from an annotated overhead frame, whether its strategies and yielders clear the jam, whether it adds information or adapts in ways no geometric rule does, and, because every decision is grounded deterministically, what the grounder contributes. Any resolution is credited to the pipeline (VLM and grounding together), not to the model alone.
